Source: shadowfetch-defaults
Section: misc
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Shadowfetch Project <maintainers@shadowfetch.com>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13)
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Homepage: https://www.shadowfetchlinux.org/

Package: shadowfetch-defaults
Architecture: all
Depends:
 ${misc:Depends},
# /usr/bin/shadowfetch-workbench (this package) reads its package records from
# /usr/share/shadowfetch/welcome/catalog/workbench-*.json and installs them
# through /usr/libexec/shadowfetch-bundle-install -- both shipped by
# shadowfetch-welcome, which nothing here named. Every profile the Workbench
# offers exits with 'Profile package record is unavailable' on an install that
# has this package and not that one, so the relation is Depends, not Recommends.
 shadowfetch-welcome (= ${binary:Version}),
 ca-certificates,
 curl,
 fastfetch,
 iproute2,
 konsole,
 libnotify-bin,
 pciutils,
 pkexec,
 polkitd,
 podman,
 psmisc,
 python3,
 python3-pyqt6,
 sudo,
 vulkan-tools,
 wl-clipboard,
 xclip,
 xdg-utils
Recommends:
# /usr/bin/shadowfetch-update (this package) is a compatibility shim that
# execs /usr/bin/fireproof and does nothing else. Without shadowfetch-
# fireproof it prints where updates live and exits 1 -- honest, but useless.
# Recommends, not Depends: a headless install that never updates through the
# desktop path is still a working system, and a Depends here would drag the
# update daemon onto machines that manage packages with plain apt.
 shadowfetch-fireproof,
 shadowfetch-themes,
 shadowfetch-branding,
# /usr/bin/shadowfetch-passport (this package) runs shadowfetch-hwscan for the
# hardware section of the Passport and omits that section when it is absent.
# Undeclared until now.
 shadowfetch-hwscan,
 papirus-icon-theme,
 snapper,
 grub-btrfs
# /usr/bin/shadowfetch-doctor (this package) diagnoses the pillars: it runs
# shadowfetch-firebreak and shadowfetch-checkpoint, reads the mission engine's
# module directory and Phoenix's apt-recovery payload. Its own rule is that a
# check it could not perform reports SKIP and never PASS, so none of these is a
# Depends -- but the reach was undeclared, and a diagnostic tool that quietly
# skips half the system because the graph never said what it inspects is the
# failure mode it exists to prevent.
Suggests:
 shadowfetch-fireline,
 shadowfetch-missions,
 shadowfetch-phoenix,
# /usr/lib/shadowfetch/desktop/sf_desktop.py (this package, W-30) is the one
# desktop library both front-ends import. One function in it,
# load_ember_profiles(), reads /usr/share/shadowfetch/ember/profiles, and the
# .conf cards in that directory are shipped by shadowfetch-control-center. It
# has exactly one caller -- sfcc/ember_page.py -- and returns [] when the
# directory is absent, so nothing in this package needs Mission Control
# installed. Suggests, therefore, and not Recommends: this is a declaration
# that the reach exists (tools/ship_list_check.py fails an undeclared one),
# not a claim that the two are useful together.
#
# It is also a leftover. The Ember profile reader is not one of W-30's five
# shared facts and belongs beside its only caller; when it moves to
# sfcc/ember_page.py this line goes with it.
 shadowfetch-control-center
Description: Shadowfetch default user configuration
 Ships the Shadowfetch defaults applied to new user accounts (via
 /etc/skel): kdeglobals, kwinrc, konsole profile, fastfetch config.
 Also ships a system-wide /etc/profile.d snippet that prints a
 fastfetch banner on the first interactive shell, plus the Shadowfetch
 Control Center, safe updater, health diagnostics and project workspace tools.
